# Sway config -- stow-managed from ~/Desktop/configs/sway/config.
# Edit it here, not at ~/.config/sway/config (that is a symlink into this repo).
# Apply changes with `swaymsg reload` or $mod+Ctrl+c.
#
# Binding grammar (adopted 2026-08-22, DECISIONS.md item 6). The modifier names
# what the action acts on -- to place or recall a binding, ask what it acts on:
#
#   $mod          you: your attention and your instruments. Move focus
#                 (hjkl/arrows), visit workspaces (1-0, Tab), summon things
#                 (launchers, menu, scratchpad show), toggle your equipment
#                 (w bar, m touchpad).
#   $mod+Shift    the focused window: move it (hjkl/arrows), send it (1-0),
#                 stash it (minus), toggle its state (f fullscreen,
#                 space floating).
#   $mod+Ctrl     the session: l lock, c reload.
#   $mod+$alt     the workspace row: walk it (left/right); +Shift carries the
#                 focused window along, so Shift keeps its meaning composed.
#
# Two clauses complete the law:
#
#   * Key families override the table on their own key. Escape means LEAVING,
#     graded by weight: $mod+Escape ends the session, +Shift reaches the
#     machine (poweroff/reboot nag). Print means capture; XF86* means
#     hardware. The clause is forced by Print regardless (Shift+Print is a
#     capture variant, no window involved), so Escape's gradient rides it for
#     free.
#
#   * Promotions are a stated, closed list: q kill and r resize are window
#     verbs sitting one tier light because they are pressed all day. The
#     default home for a new window verb is Shift; promotion is earned by
#     daily frequency and recorded here.
#
# Placing a new binding: an app -> $mod+mnemonic letter ($mod+v is
# deliberately free). A window verb -> $mod+Shift+letter. A session op ->
# $mod+Ctrl+letter. A machine action -> a button on the $mod+Shift+Escape nag
# before it ever claims a chord. Hardware with no XF86 key -> a bare-tier
# toggle letter (m, the touchpad, is the precedent). The container tier
# (splits, focus parent/child, layouts) is deliberately absent -- see the
# Layout section.
#
# Both vim keys and arrow keys are bound for every motion, on purpose -- the
# parity is maintained deliberately, so add arrow variants to any new motion.
#
# Caution when editing: `sway --validate` does NOT check the command body of a
# bindsym. A typo there passes validation and fails silently at runtime. Verify
# bindings by running the command directly: `swaymsg -- <the command>`.
#
# Read `man 5 sway` for a complete reference.


### Variables

set $mod Mod4
set $alt Mod1

# Home row direction keys, like vim.
set $left h
set $down j
set $up k
set $right l

set $term wezterm
set $menu wofi --show drun


### Output configuration

# `~` is expanded here (sway runs background paths through wordexp), and an
# unreadable path is a hard config error rather than a silent fallback, so a
# typo in this path fails loudly at load.
output * bg ~/Desktop/configs/wallpapers/monarch1080dark.png fill

# Additional outputs are auto-enabled and arranged left-to-right in connection
# order. To pin one:  output HDMI-A-1 resolution 1920x1080 position 1920,0
# Output names come from `swaymsg -t get_outputs`.


### Input configuration

# This block is LIVE, not an example. Devices can also be addressed
# individually rather than by type -- see `man 5 sway-input`.
input type:touchpad {
    dwt enabled                 # disable-while-typing
    tap enabled                 # tap to click
    natural_scroll enabled
    middle_emulation enabled
}


### Key bindings

#
# Session, power, and the bar
#
    # Reload re-reads this config: a session op, so Ctrl tier, c = config. It
    # sat on $mod+Shift+c (upstream's default) until 2026-08-22; only the
    # modifier moved, so that Shift could mean the focused window and nothing
    # else.
    bindsym $mod+Ctrl+c reload

    # swaynag gotcha: only -z/-Z buttons dismiss the nag; -b/-B run their action
    # and leave the bar on screen. The dismiss button is built in -- `-s` only
    # renames it, which is how "Cancel" below is made to actually cancel.
    # Use -B rather than -b: -b routes the action through $TERMINAL when that
    # variable is set, which is not wanted for either of these.
    bindsym $mod+Escape exec swaynag -t warning -s 'Cancel' \
        -m 'You pressed the exit shortcut. Do you really want to exit sway? This will end your Wayland session.' \
        -B 'Yes, exit sway' 'swaymsg exit'

    # Machine actions share this one nag rather than each claiming a chord
    # (Escape family: leaving, graded -- the session on $mod+Escape, the
    # machine one key heavier). Reboot joined as a second button 2026-08-22,
    # settling a parked $mod+Shift+r idea that predated the nag.
    bindsym $mod+Shift+Escape exec swaynag -t warning -s 'Cancel' \
        -m 'Shut down or reboot?' \
        -B 'Poweroff' 'systemctl poweroff' \
        -B 'Reboot' 'systemctl reboot'

    # Lock on demand: a session op, so Ctrl tier, l = lock. (Not $mod+l: that
    # is `focus right` ($right = l) -- the same collision that moved the exit
    # binding off $mod+Shift+l on 2025-11-10.)
    # Appearance is in the swaylock package, not inline -- see swaylock/config.
    bindsym $mod+Ctrl+l exec swaylock -f

    # Toggle Waybar. Sway's own bar hiding (`mode hide`) does not work here
    # because Waybar ignores sway's bar protocol and reads its own config, so
    # the toggle goes through Waybar's SIGUSR1 handler instead. See ### Status
    # bar below for why Waybar is launched as sway's bar in the first place.
    bindsym $mod+w exec killall -SIGUSR1 waybar

#
# Launchers
#
    bindsym $mod+Return exec $term
    bindsym $mod+t      exec $term
    bindsym $mod+a      exec $menu
    bindsym $mod+b      exec brave-browser
    bindsym $mod+c      exec google-chrome-stable
    bindsym $mod+d      exec nautilus --new-window ~/Desktop
    bindsym $mod+f      exec nautilus --new-window
    bindsym $mod+x      exec gnome-text-editor

#
# Windows
#
    bindsym $mod+q kill

    # Make the focused window fullscreen
    bindsym $mod+Shift+f fullscreen

    # Toggle the focused window between tiling and floating
    bindsym $mod+Shift+space floating toggle

    # Swap focus between the tiling area and the floating area
    bindsym $mod+space focus mode_toggle

    # Drag floating windows with $mod + left mouse button, resize with $mod +
    # right. Despite the name this also works on tiled windows. `inverse` swaps
    # the two buttons.
    floating_modifier $mod normal

#
# Focus
#
    bindsym $mod+$left  focus left
    bindsym $mod+$down  focus down
    bindsym $mod+$up    focus up
    bindsym $mod+$right focus right

    bindsym $mod+Left   focus left
    bindsym $mod+Down   focus down
    bindsym $mod+Up     focus up
    bindsym $mod+Right  focus right

    # focus parent / focus child are deliberately unbound: the container tier
    # went unused and was dropped 2026-08-22. The reasoning and the revival
    # chords are recorded in the Layout section below.

#
# Moving windows
#
    bindsym $mod+Shift+$left  move left
    bindsym $mod+Shift+$down  move down
    bindsym $mod+Shift+$up    move up
    bindsym $mod+Shift+$right move right

    bindsym $mod+Shift+Left   move left
    bindsym $mod+Shift+Down   move down
    bindsym $mod+Shift+Up     move up
    bindsym $mod+Shift+Right  move right

#
# Workspaces
#
    # Walk workspaces on the current output. prev/next_on_output cycle only
    # among workspaces that actually exist, so they skip unopened numbers.
    bindsym $mod+$alt+$left  workspace prev_on_output
    bindsym $mod+$alt+$right workspace next_on_output
    bindsym $mod+$alt+Left   workspace prev_on_output
    bindsym $mod+$alt+Right  workspace next_on_output

    # Same, carrying the focused window along. `move container` does not follow
    # focus, hence the second command in each chain. Verified correct including
    # the case where the move empties (and so destroys) the current workspace.
    bindsym $mod+$alt+Shift+$left  move container to workspace prev_on_output; workspace prev_on_output
    bindsym $mod+$alt+Shift+$right move container to workspace next_on_output; workspace next_on_output
    bindsym $mod+$alt+Shift+Left   move container to workspace prev_on_output; workspace prev_on_output
    bindsym $mod+$alt+Shift+Right  move container to workspace next_on_output; workspace next_on_output

    # Bounce back to the previously focused workspace. The third motion the
    # $alt prev/next pair leaves missing: those two walk the row, this one
    # returns to wherever you just were regardless of distance.
    bindsym $mod+Tab workspace back_and_forth

    # Jump to a workspace by number. Workspaces can carry any name, not just
    # numbers; 1-10 is simply the default scheme.
    bindsym $mod+1 workspace number 1
    bindsym $mod+2 workspace number 2
    bindsym $mod+3 workspace number 3
    bindsym $mod+4 workspace number 4
    bindsym $mod+5 workspace number 5
    bindsym $mod+6 workspace number 6
    bindsym $mod+7 workspace number 7
    bindsym $mod+8 workspace number 8
    bindsym $mod+9 workspace number 9
    bindsym $mod+0 workspace number 10

    # Send the focused window to a workspace by number
    bindsym $mod+Shift+1 move container to workspace number 1
    bindsym $mod+Shift+2 move container to workspace number 2
    bindsym $mod+Shift+3 move container to workspace number 3
    bindsym $mod+Shift+4 move container to workspace number 4
    bindsym $mod+Shift+5 move container to workspace number 5
    bindsym $mod+Shift+6 move container to workspace number 6
    bindsym $mod+Shift+7 move container to workspace number 7
    bindsym $mod+Shift+8 move container to workspace number 8
    bindsym $mod+Shift+9 move container to workspace number 9
    bindsym $mod+Shift+0 move container to workspace number 10

#
# Layout -- deliberately empty: the container tier is unbound
#
    # Sway's middle tier -- the containers between window and workspace -- goes
    # unused in this workflow, and its bindings are absent on purpose, not
    # forgotten. Two dated decisions:
    #
    # * Tabbed/stacking layouts, unbound 2026-08-13: the keybindings were
    #   clunky and the feature went unused, since every application that needs
    #   tabs here brings its own (WezTerm, browsers, Okular). Separate from
    #   `workspace_layout default` below, which only governs how *new*
    #   workspaces start.
    #
    # * splith/splitv ($mod+Ctrl+h/v) and focus parent/child ($mod+p,
    #   $mod+Shift+p), dropped 2026-08-22 once their use-cases were laid out:
    #   splits had never been pressed intentionally, parent/child never at
    #   all. With one or two windows per workspace -- the norm on this
    #   laptop's 13" panel -- a workspace stays a flat row of leaves, and the
    #   group that focus parent would select coincides with the workspace
    #   itself. Supersedes the 2026-08-13 decision that kept the split pair
    #   on Ctrl+h/v.
    #
    # What stays reachable without any of it, measured in a nested sway
    # 2026-08-22: new windows tile side by side on their own, and an
    # orthogonal $mod+Shift+j/k move still builds stacked layouts implicitly
    # -- two 640x720 columns became two full-width 1280x360 rows on one `move
    # down`. (Also where the occasional accidental vertical split always came
    # from.)
    #
    # If tree work ever becomes real -- plausibly a big-screen sway on bigfed,
    # where three-plus windows per workspace and deliberate arrangements would
    # be the norm -- revive the tier as: splits back on $mod+Ctrl+h/v, focus
    # parent/child on the bare (attention) tier, layout ops on Ctrl letters.
    # $mod+Ctrl then widens from "the session" to "the enclosures: the
    # container around the window and the session around everything".

#
# Scratchpad
#
    # The scratchpad is a bag of holding for windows: send one away, get it back
    # later. With several inside, `scratchpad show` cycles through them.
    bindsym $mod+Shift+minus move scratchpad
    bindsym $mod+minus scratchpad show

#
# Hardware and media
#
    # --locked keeps these working while swaylock is up, so volume and
    # brightness stay adjustable on a locked machine.
    bindsym --locked XF86AudioMute        exec pactl set-sink-mute \@DEFAULT_SINK@ toggle
    bindsym --locked XF86AudioLowerVolume exec pactl set-sink-volume \@DEFAULT_SINK@ -5%
    bindsym --locked XF86AudioRaiseVolume exec pactl set-sink-volume \@DEFAULT_SINK@ +5%
    bindsym --locked XF86AudioMicMute     exec pactl set-source-mute \@DEFAULT_SOURCE@ toggle

    bindsym --locked XF86MonBrightnessDown exec brightnessctl set 5%-
    bindsym --locked XF86MonBrightnessUp   exec brightnessctl set 5%+

    # Toggle the trackpad.
    bindsym $mod+m input type:touchpad events toggle enabled disabled

#
# Screenshots
#
    # Whole screen -> file, landing in ~/Pictures as YYYYMMDD_HHhMMmSSs_grim.png.
    #
    # Measured 2026-08-13, because the man page misleads here: grim honours
    # $GRIM_DEFAULT_DIR, but the **$XDG_PICTURES_DIR environment variable is
    # ignored** -- setting it to a scratch directory still wrote to ~/Pictures.
    # grim reads the pictures directory from ~/.config/user-dirs.dirs instead, so
    # the man page's "falls back first to $XDG_PICTURES_DIR" names the value, not
    # the variable. Two consequences: `export XDG_PICTURES_DIR=...` would do
    # nothing, and exporting GRIM_DEFAULT_DIR from bash/.bashrc.d would not reach
    # this binding either -- sway's children inherit the *session* environment,
    # not an interactive shell's. Redirecting it would mean naming a path here.
    bindsym Print exec grim

    # Region -> clipboard, and region -> file. slurp prints the geometry; Esc
    # cancels it.
    #
    # The guard is not decoration. Written the obvious way as
    # `grim -g "$(slurp)" - | wl-copy`, cancelling the selection leaves an empty
    # geometry, grim fails, and wl-copy still runs with nothing on stdin -- which
    # silently WIPES the clipboard. Testing both the exit status and the string
    # means a cancelled screenshot changes nothing at all.
    bindsym Shift+Print exec sh -c 'g=$(slurp) && [ -n "$g" ] && grim -g "$g" - | wl-copy'
    bindsym $mod+Print  exec sh -c 'g=$(slurp) && [ -n "$g" ] && grim -g "$g"'

#
# Resize mode
#
mode "resize" {
    # left/right shrink and grow width; up/down shrink and grow height
    bindsym $left  resize shrink width 10px
    bindsym $down  resize grow height 10px
    bindsym $up    resize shrink height 10px
    bindsym $right resize grow width 10px

    bindsym Left   resize shrink width 10px
    bindsym Down   resize grow height 10px
    bindsym Up     resize shrink height 10px
    bindsym Right  resize grow width 10px

    # Return to default mode
    bindsym Return mode "default"
    bindsym Escape mode "default"
}
bindsym $mod+r mode "resize"


### Status bar

# Waybar is launched as sway's bar rather than via `exec_always waybar`. The
# trade-off, deliberately taken: sway respawns Waybar if it dies and there is no
# duplicate-process problem on reload, but sway's own bar settings (position,
# mode, hidden_state, colors, font) are dead config here -- Waybar ignores the
# bar protocol and reads ~/.config/waybar/config instead. So do not add settings
# to this block expecting them to take effect; change Waybar's own config.
# Sway appends `-b bar-0` to this command; Waybar ignores an unmatched bar id.
# Visibility is toggled with $mod+w -- see "Session, power, and the bar" above.
bar {
    swaybar_command waybar
}


### Appearance

# No titlebars; a thicker outline carries the focus signal instead.
default_border pixel 3              # tiling windows: border, no titlebar
default_floating_border pixel 3     # floating windows too

# Hide borders when a workspace holds only one tiled window.
smart_borders on

# Start new workspaces in split layout (no tab bar).
workspace_layout default

# Palette.
# $accent is the live one: dodger blue. $forest is the retired forest-green
# accent, kept so it can be swapped back by changing a single word below. Note
# that Waybar is still on forest green (waybar/style.css), so the two
# deliberately differ.
set $forest #228B22
set $accent #0088FF

# $urgent is shared with the rest of the desktop on purpose: Waybar's urgent
# workspace, mako's critical border, and swaylock's wrong-password ring all use
# this same colour, so "something needs you" looks identical everywhere.
set $urgent #d08770

# Format: border  background  text  indicator  child_border
client.focused          $accent  #000000 #ffffff $accent $accent
client.focused_inactive #5f676a  #000000 #ffffff #5f676a #5f676a
client.unfocused        #434c5e  #000000 #ffffff #434c5e #434c5e
client.urgent           $urgent  #000000 #ffffff $urgent $urgent


### Deliberate omissions
#
# Recorded so they are not mistaken for oversights and "fixed" later:
#
# * No idle timers and no screen blanking. The screen stays on until dimmed by
#   hand, because sway sessions here are long stretches of focused work. This is
#   the same posture as leaving Caffeine on under GNOME. Note that the swayidle
#   line under "Includes and startup" is NOT a timer -- it has no `timeout`
#   clause at all, only `before-sleep`, so it can never blank or lock mid-work.
#
#   The hole this deliberately leaves closed as of 2026-08-13: logind's default
#   HandleLidSwitch=suspend applies, so closing the lid suspends, and before
#   2026-08-13 reopening it returned straight to an unlocked desktop (verified
#   by test). The before-sleep hook now locks first.
#
# * No tabbed or stacking container layouts -- see the Layout section above.


### Includes and startup

include /etc/sway/config.d/*

# Lock the screen before the machine suspends -- and ONLY that. There is no
# `timeout` clause, so this never blanks or locks during work; see "Deliberate
# omissions" above. Without it, logind's HandleLidSwitch=suspend meant closing
# the lid and reopening returned straight to an unlocked desktop.
#
# `-w` (swayidle) plus `-f` (swaylock) is the pairing the swayidle man page
# prescribes: -f detaches only once the screen is actually locked, and -w makes
# swayidle hold logind's inhibitor until then, so the machine cannot suspend
# with the desktop still visible. logind caps that wait at InhibitDelayMaxSec
# (5s here); swaylock comes up well inside it.
#
# `exec`, not `exec_always`: a reload must not spawn a second swayidle.
exec swayidle -w before-sleep 'swaylock -f'

# NetworkManager secret agent -- pulls 802.1x creds from gnome-keyring.
# Fedora's /etc/xdg/autostart/nm-applet.desktop is meant to autostart it
# under Sway via sway-xdg-autostart.target, but doesn't reliably fire here.
# `exec`, not `exec_always`, so a reload does not stack up applets.
exec nm-applet --indicator
